WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.403
Collections represent groups of values.

00:00:02.553 --> 00:00:07.108
A List preserves an ordered sequence, a Set represents uniqueness, and a

00:00:07.108 --> 00:00:09.006
Map associates keys with values.

00:00:09.156 --> 00:00:12.926
Equality and hash-code contracts matter when objects become keys.

00:00:13.076 --> 00:00:17.305
Mutating a field used in hashCode after insertion can make a key

00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:19.420
effectively unreachable in a hash-based collection.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.105
Generics express element types and reduce unsafe casts.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:28.633
Streams describe transformations such as filtering, mapping, grouping, and reduction.

00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:32.970
They are not automatically faster than loops, and a stream pipeline should

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.366
avoid hidden side effects.

00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:39.143
Parallel streams use shared execution resources and are usually a poor default

00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:41.456
for blocking database or HTTP work.

00:00:41.606 --> 00:00:45.989
JavaBasics.quantities groups basket lines by SKU and sums quantities.

00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:49.589
Two lines for SKU a with quantities two and three become a

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:51.027
single entry with quantity five.

00:00:51.177 --> 00:00:54.777
The total function reduces line totals with BigDecimal.ZERO.

00:00:54.927 --> 00:00:58.374
These operations are pure enough to test without starting Spring.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:01.338
Build a report of the three highest-selling SKUs.

00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:06.558
Define tie ordering, null handling, and whether returned collections may be modified.

00:01:06.708 --> 00:01:10.794
Compare a loop implementation with a stream implementation for readability.

00:01:10.944 --> 00:01:15.244
Use deterministic sorting, such as descending quantity followed by SKU.

00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:19.785
Reject invalid domain data early instead of scattering null checks through the

00:01:19.785 --> 00:01:20.151
pipeline.

00:01:20.301 --> 00:01:24.316
Return a copied or immutable collection when callers should not mutate the

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:24.650
report.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:25.472
Question.

00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:27.760
Why must equals and hashCode agree?

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:28.487
Answer.

00:01:28.637 --> 00:01:32.591
Equal objects must have equal hash codes so hash-based lookup can find

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:33.580
an equal key.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:34.401
Question.

00:01:34.551 --> 00:01:36.480
When is a loop preferable to a stream?

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.207
Answer.

00:01:37.357 --> 00:01:42.243
When early exits, complex branching, or explicit state make the loop easier

00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:43.872
to understand and verify.
